From: vyos-ls1046a-build CI <ci@local>
Date: Tue, 01 Sep 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fman: port: fix O/H port discoverability before first bring-up (phase 1c fix)

Board-tested 2026-09-01 on .185 (image vyos-2026.09.01-1737-rolling,
commit deab7141): "echo 'bringup 4' > /sys/kernel/debug/
fman_port_oh_test/bringup" failed with ENODEV ("no such O/H port
(never probed?)"), even though hw port 0x04 was confirmed bound
(/sys/bus/platform/devices/1a84000.port/driver -> fsl-fman-port).

Root cause: 0177's bringup handler searches fman_port_active_list,
which fman_port_init() populates -- but nothing had ever called
fman_port_init() for this port (that's the whole point of the
bringup tool), so the list was empty for it. Chicken-and-egg: the
lookup only finds ports that have already been through the exact
sequence the tool exists to run for the first time.

Fix, two parts:
1. fman_port_probe() now registers O/H ports into
   fman_port_active_list at PROBE time, not init time. Safe to add
   without touching RX/TX: they still register (a second time) at
   init time as before, unaffected; O/H has no other consumer that
   will ever call fman_port_init() for it, so probe time is the only
   sensible registration point. port->node is kzalloc'd/zeroed at
   this point, which list_add_tail() handles fine (it only writes
   through the node, never reads it as an existing link).
2. The bringup handler's lookup now matches on dts_params.id/.type
   instead of port_id/port_type. The latter two are only populated
   by fman_port_config() itself -- before that call they're still
   zero, so matching on them would have kept failing even with fix
   1 alone. dts_params is set at probe time and never changes, so
   it's valid at every point in an O/H port's life. (This is exactly
   why fman_port_lookup_oh()'s own port_id/port_type match is
   correct for ITS contract -- finding an already-initialized port
   -- but was the wrong thing to copy into a pre-init lookup.)

Not yet re-tested on hardware after this fix (queued for the next
board session). Calling bringup twice for the same port without a
reboot between calls now also leaves a second, duplicate
fman_port_active_list entry for the same pointer (in addition to
the already-documented port->cfg leak) -- harmless (same pointer
both times, any future is-this-a-TX-port-style list walk still just
sees it twice and skips it on the type filter) but still a
single-use-per-boot debug tool, not a repeatable one.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate | Risk-Tier: A (fixes newly-added debug-only tooling from 0177/0176; no RX/TX/CC/ehash/HMTD path touched)
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c | 32 +++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 31 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c
index e8a7678a6..3142258cc 100644
--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/freescale/fman/fman_port.c
@@ -2600,6 +2600,24 @@ static int fman_port_probe(struct platform_device *of_dev)

 	dev_set_drvdata(&of_dev->dev, port);

+	/*
+	 * ASK2 OH-port re-enqueue infra (dpaa1): RX/TX ports join the
+	 * active-port registry (0115, fman_port_active_list) inside
+	 * fman_port_init(), once a netdev consumer has driven them through
+	 * fman_port_config()+fman_port_init(). O/H ports have no such
+	 * consumer -- nothing else will ever call config()/init() for one,
+	 * a debugfs/production caller has to find it FIRST in order to do
+	 * so. Register here at probe time instead, so a probed-but-not-yet-
+	 * brought-up O/H port is still discoverable. Safe: port->node is
+	 * kzalloc'd (zeroed), and list_add_tail() only writes through it,
+	 * never reads it as an existing link first.
+	 */
+	if (port_type == FMAN_PORT_TYPE_OH) {
+		mutex_lock(&fman_port_active_lock);
+		list_add_tail(&port->node, &fman_port_active_list);
+		mutex_unlock(&fman_port_active_lock);
+	}
+
 	return 0;

 put_device:
@@ -2659,10 +2677,22 @@ static ssize_t fman_port_oh_test_write(struct file *file,
 	if (sscanf(kbuf, "bringup %hhu", &port_id) != 1)
 		return -EINVAL;

+	/*
+	 * Match on dts_params.id/.type, NOT port_id/port_type: those two
+	 * are only populated by fman_port_config(), which this function
+	 * exists to call for the FIRST time -- before that, they're still
+	 * zero from kzalloc(). dts_params is populated at probe time and
+	 * never changes, so it's the only field valid at every point in an
+	 * O/H port's life, whether or not fman_port_config() has run yet.
+	 * (Found this the hard way: the port_id/port_type match here
+	 * originally mirrored fman_port_lookup_oh()'s -- correct for THAT
+	 * function's post-init contract, wrong for this pre-init one.)
+	 */
 	port = NULL;
 	mutex_lock(&fman_port_active_lock);
 	list_for_each_entry(p, &fman_port_active_list, node) {
-		if (p->port_id == port_id && p->port_type == FMAN_PORT_TYPE_OH) {
+		if (p->dts_params.id == port_id &&
+		    p->dts_params.type == FMAN_PORT_TYPE_OH) {
 			port = p;
 			break;
 		}
